WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.620
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gold standard;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.100
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.800
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.900
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
I do!

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.320
It’s time for the
Nutrition Facts Grab Bag

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.020
where we look at the science
on a wide variety of topics.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.900
Today we start with
the results of a study

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.740
that has correlated low calcium intake
and vitamin D exposure

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.560
with higher bone fracture rates
in British vegans.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.120
Vegetarians had slightly lower bone
mineral density in their spines.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.600
Although the difference was
basically within the margin of error

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.520
for the test, if the bone quality

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.640
really is compromised, it could
lead to collapsed vertebrae,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.640
increased spinal fracture risk,
but there's no evidence for this.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.180
The incidence of vertebral fracture
was ascertained in older women

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.620
who had been vegan for most of
their lives -- 34 years on average.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.180
And despite their calcium intake
being terrible, about half that

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.480
of the nonvegans, and a quarter
of them vitamin D deficient,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.560
the incidence of vertebral fractures
was not significantly different.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.740
Although the vegans had a higher
prevalence of vitamin D deficiency

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.040
and lower dietary calcium
intakes, the two factors

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.960
were not associated
with bone loss.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.260
In fact, the annual loss in bone
mineral density in the hips

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.440
of vegans was less than
half that of the meat eaters,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.260
though the difference did not
reach statistical significance.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.620
Vegetarian women had not been
found to be at higher risk

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.500
of any kind of fractures including
wrist fractures in this case,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.160
though among vegetarians,
those who consumed the least

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.520
vegetable protein intake were
at the highest risk for fracture.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.820
Those who ate beans every day,
or nuts, or like veggie burgers

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.060
only had a third of the wrist fractures
compared to vegetarians

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.580
who only ate beans or
other higher protein foods

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.900
less than three times a week.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.620
So those that consume a vegan
or vegetarian diet may be

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.160
at increased risk of fracture unless
care is taken to ensure an adequate

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.360
quantity and variety of foods high
in protein, such as whole grains,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.140
nuts, and beans, split peas,
chickpeas, or lentils are in the diet.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.140
That's one of the reasons
in my free Daily Dozen app

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.960
I recommend whole grains
and legumes every day.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.280
Hip fractures are even more
serious. Those eating legumes,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.860
like beans every day, reduced
their risk of hip fracture

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.080
by more than 60%, compared to
40% lower risk from meat protein,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.140
with plant-based meats
coming in in between

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.900
with about 50% lower
risk of hip fractures.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.140
What's the bottom line
on plant-based diets and

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.940
bone health according
to this 2020 review?

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.780
Theoretically, a long-term
plant-based diet may reduce

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.140
the risk of osteoporosis, but
that has yet to be demonstrated.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.740
What we do know is that
plant-based diets, when ensuring

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.660
adequate calcium and vitamin D levels,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.720
don't appear to have any
detrimental effects on bone health.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.620
But this was published August 2020.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.460
In November 2020, the 12-year
follow-up to the study I talked about

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.760
in my last video on comparative
fracture risk in vegetarians

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.960
versus nonvegetarians

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.380
was published, finding that
non-meat eaters, especially vegans,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.540
had higher risks of
total bone fractures,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.020
including at sites associated with
osteoporosis such as hip fractures.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.520
It comes out to be about 20 more
cases in vegans for every 1000

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.660
people over 10 years.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.280
So if indeed this is cause-and-effect,
eating vegan there would be

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.340
an annual 1 in 500 chance
of having a bone fracture

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.840
that you otherwise
might not have had.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.660
Was it because they weren't
eating enough beans?

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.420
Apparently not, since
vegans getting more protein

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.160
still apparently had higher risk.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.340
Maybe it was because they
weren't getting enough calcium?

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.100
Apparently not, since vegans
getting more calcium

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.740
still apparently had higher risk.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.660
What about bone and vitamin B12?
If you remember EPIC-Oxford,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.340
where the bone data comes from,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.780
is the same group of British vegans
who had rampant B12 deficiency.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.800
More than half the vegans were
B12 deficient because they

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.760
weren't adequately supplementing
with B12 or B12-fortified foods.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.640
This can lead to high
homocysteine levels,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.860
which not only increase stroke risk,

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.040
but may increase the
activity of bone-eating cells.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.020
This was in a petri dish, but you do
indeed see low bone mineral density

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.440
in those born with a birth defect
that leads to high homocysteine

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.180
levels in the blood. Therefore,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.780
high serum homocysteine
may be regarded as a factor

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.940
that can reduce both
bone mass and quality,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.620
but you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.340
And homocysteine-lowering
treatment failed to reduce

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.880
the risk of bone fracture.
So in the end, the effect of B12

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.760
deficiency in bone health
remains to be established.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.280
OK, so how do we explain the higher
fracture rates found among vegans?

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.340
The investigators conclude that their
findings suggest that bone health

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.000
in vegans requires further research,
but there were some clues.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.360
The elevated fracture risk,
both for total fractures

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.220
and for hip fractures specifically,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.720
was only significant for
those under a BMI of 22.5,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.560
which is like under about 130 pounds
for a woman of average height.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.940
So part of the problem is that vegans
tend to be so slender on average.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.020
Why are overweight and obese
individuals protected from fractures?

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.980
Well, think about it. They have
cushioning during a fall.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.660
There's more of a
cushion on your hips.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.440
Also, there's an enzyme in fatty
tissue that churns out estrogen,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.380
which is why women increase
their breast cancer risk

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.680
a percentage point for every
pound they gain in adulthood.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.040
But estrogen can also have
a bone-preserving effect.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.700
Now you can get the best of both
worlds consuming soy foods,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.880
preventing bone loss while
at the same time associated

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.000
with lessening breast
cancer risk for both estrogen

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.820
receptor positive tumors and
estrogen receptor negative tumors.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.780
Finally, overweight and obese
individuals may also have

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.480
stronger bones just from
the increased weight-bearing.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.300
Carrying 100 extra pounds,
you're doing major

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.820
weight bearing exercise
just walking across the room.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.900
So the risk difference they saw
between vegans and meat eaters

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.100
were likely at least partially
due to the difference in BMI.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.520
My money, however,
is on vitamin D.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.300
Great Britain is at
Canadian latitudes.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.940
The sun's rays are
at such an angle

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.000
during the winter months up there
that the vitamin D levels among

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.200
British vegans in the wintertime
drops down to suboptimal levels.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.500
Ideally, we should be up around
75 nanomoles per liter,

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.100
or 30 nanograms per milliliter
depending on what units you're using,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:40.500
which the vegans nail in the summer.
It is the sunshine vitamin after all.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.100
But in the winter, not getting
the vitamin D added to dairy or

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.240
found naturally in oily fish, if vegans
aren't supplementing at that latitude

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.360
during the winter, their vitamin
D levels may drop too low.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.320
Now randomized controlled trials
show that vitamin D alone

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.780
does not seem to
reduce fracture rates,

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:01.560
but boosting people's D and
calcium at the same time does.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.240
So maybe it was a combination of the
relatively low D and calcium intakes

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.880
among the vegans that led
to their higher fracture rates.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.940
We won't know for sure
until it's actually put to the test,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.571
and when it is you can be sure
I'll do a video about it.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.640
Did you know that our body
can make vitamin K2

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.920
from the K1 in green
leafy vegetables?

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.840
Here’s the story.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.520
Vitamin K.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.480
Wait, I know about
vitamins A, B, C, D, and E.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.780
What happened to
vitamins F, G, H, I and J?

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.700
It's not alphabetical.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:41.040
Vitamin K stands for coagulation,
or at least it does in German.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.080
That's the fundamental role vitamin K
plays: helping the blood to clot.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.260
But over the last few decades there's
evidence that it has other roles

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.720
in the health of our
bones, heart, and brain.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:54.180
Kind of reminds me of vitamin D.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.580
We know vitamin D is important
for bone health, but then

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.700
there's been all sorts of other
controversial functions ascribed

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:03.060
to it, some of which have been
proven and some disproven.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.860
What about vitamin K?

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.480
For bone health, for example,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.440
is the link between vitamin K
and osteoporosis myth or reality?

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.060
It turns out the findings on vitamin K
and bone are conflicting and unclear.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.700
It doesn't help that
some of the major trials

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:22.080
were found to be problematic
to say the least, as in...

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.240
likely fraudulent,
containing impossible data,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.260
with investigators admitting
to complete fabrication.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:35.160
And so if you do a systematic
review, eliminating any fraud,

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:38.580
we find no evidence that vitamin K
supplementation affects

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.920
bone mineral density
or vertebral fractures.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:42.720
What about the heart?

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.980
Vitamin K supplementation for the
prevention of cardiovascular disease.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.920
There's a vitamin K-activated
protein in your blood

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.840
that binds up excess calcium

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.840
and helps prevent calcium from
being deposited into the walls

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.280
of your arteries and
stiffening them.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.680
So if you give people
extra vitamin K,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.500
will that protect peoples'
arteries from calcification?

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:06.840
It sounds good in theory, but no,
vitamin K does not appear

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.680
to consistently prevent progression
of calcification, atherosclerosis,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.180
or arterial stiffness.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.840
For example, artery calcification
is particularly common in patients

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.200
with chronic kidney disease, which
can lead to increased artery stiffness,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.020
which is an important risk factor
for heart attacks and strokes.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:26.700
An earlier trial didn't find any benefit
on coronary artery calcification

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.680
between the vitamin K
and placebo groups,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.360
but they were using
kind of a small dose.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:35.820
So these trials used a whopping
dose daily for a year and... nada.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.480
Vitamin K supplementation did
not improve vascular stiffness

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.040
or other measures of artery health.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.520
And in fact, one study on the effect
of vitamin K supplementation

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.760
on artery calcification in patients
with diabetes found that

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.820
calcification tended to increase
after supplementation

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.820
with a type of vitamin K
found in a slimy fermented

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.140
soy food called natto.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.290
Now those with higher levels
of vitamin K circulating

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.520
in their bloodstreams

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.840
do tend to have lower levels
of inflammation, but no wonder.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.220
Where is vitamin K found?

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.140
The predominant dietary form

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.900
of vitamin K in the
human diet comes from

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.260
dark green leafy vegetables
and cruciferous vegetables.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.960
So how did people get
high levels in their blood?

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.340
Eating broccoli.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.980
Those with higher levels
of vitamin K in their blood

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.740
were eating more
vegetables, less meat.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.740
No wonder they had lower
levels of inflammation.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:30.120
The recommended adequate daily
intake for vitamin K is set at

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.980
70 micrograms a day in Europe,

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.520
and between 90 and 120 a day
here in the United States.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.720
Just two leaves of kale has over 70!

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.320
And a quarter cup of cooked
kale will get anyone all the K

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:44.520
they need for the day.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.180
Now there is vitamin K
found in meat, dairy and eggs,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.000
averaging about 5 to 10
micrograms per serving.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.340
In other words, they're even
beaten out by iceberg lettuce,

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:00.000
which is mostly water, but still
contains like 2 to 3 times

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.840
more vitamin K.
Ah, but that's vitamin K1,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.140
and what's found in animal
products is mostly vitamin K2.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.660
Do you need K2?

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.460
Apparently not.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.060
Once you get enough plant-based
K1, there's no established

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.820
requirement for K2,
because it hasn't been proven

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.460
that K2 has effects
that are different from K1.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.620
They both act the
same way in the body,

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.760
thus there's not even enough data
to take K2 into account at all.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.700
So when the recommended
adequate daily intakes are set,

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.760
they're only talking about
getting enough K1 from plants,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.440
mostly green vegetables.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:38.460
In fact most of the bone trials
that flopped used the K2

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.660
found in animal products,

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.660
and most of the failed heart
studies used K2 as well.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.740
OK, even though there is presently
a lack of randomized trial evidence

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.920
to support a beneficial role
for vitamin K in preventing

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.980
the worsening of cardiovascular
disease, or bone health,

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:54.300
what if that were to change?

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:58.680
What if all of the sudden K2 was
shown to have unique benefits?

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.360
Well, guess what?

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.000
The bacteria in your gut make K2.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.520
That's why fermented
foods have K2.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:06.840
Bacteria make it.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.320
And the bacteria in your gut not
only make it, but it gets absorbed

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.100
from your colon up into your system,
contributing a significant amount

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.680
of the human vitamin K
requirement, just in case

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.480
you miss a couple days of greens.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.460
Vitamin K1 is made by plants
and is the primary dietary form.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:26.880
Then there are a dozen or so types
of vitamin K2, which are synthesized

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.820
by bacteria, including several
types in the human gut.

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:35.220
The exception, though, is a type
of K2 called menaquinone 4, MK4,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:38.700
which is endogenously
synthesized in mammals

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.500
and therefore is found
in animal products.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:45.660
Now, I don't know if any of you
noticed, but we're mammals, too.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:49.920
It has consistently been shown
that vitamin K1 from greens is

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.680
endogenously converted
inside your body

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.260
to the vitamin K2 in animal products.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:59.340
You're made out of meat, too,
though it took until 2010

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:03.180
before we discovered the human
biosynthetic enzyme that does it.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:07.380
So no reason at all to take any
sort of vitamin K supplement.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.000
Eat your greens.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.360
In fact when K2 supplements
were looked at, researchers found

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.480
significant problems in terms of
contaminants and mislabeling.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.540
Eat your greens.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.640
Now K2 appears in higher
concentrations in certain tissues,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:24.540
including the brain.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:29.220
Again, we make K2 from
the K1 we eat in greens,

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.800
but maybe extra K2 might help?

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.260
Well, if we measure vitamin K
levels in the blood and brains

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:38.100
of centenarians --
those that live over 100 --

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:41.460
concentrations of circulating
K1 from vegetables,

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.180
but not cerebral K2,
not the K2 in the brain,

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:48.480
was positively correlated with a
wide range of cognitive measures.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:49.181
Why?

00:13:49.181 --> 00:13:52.140
Likely because they
were eating green vegetables,

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.300
and green vegetables
don't just have vitamin K.

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:57.720
Green leafy vegetables are the
most concentrated source of lutein,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.540
the eye health nutrient
that's taken up into the brain

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.260
and is associated with cognitive
performance across the life span.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:08.280
And so in these centenarians,
circulating K1 and lutein

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.930
concentrations were
highly correlated,

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:13.680
and so it's hard to tease out exactly
what in greens was so beneficial.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:17.460
It's like when you see data showing
lower circulating K1 levels in the

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.760
blood stream are associated with
increased risk of all-cause mortality,

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.540
meaning lower K1 levels were
correlated with a shorter lifespan.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.220
Well, duh.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:27.780
K1 is found in greens,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.320
and of all the dietary components
correlating with all-cause mortality,

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.840
the best evidence appears
to support the intake

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.040
of green leafy vegetables and salads
to reduce all-cause mortality.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:41.877
In other words, eat your greens.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.360
In our final story, we look
at how might we replicate

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.760
one of the greatest public health
victories of all time—

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:53.498
the reduction of smoking rates—
but in the field of nutrition.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.340
What we learned from
the tobacco experience,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.620
wrote two preeminent
public health scholars,

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:03.300
is how powerfully profits can
motivate “even at the cost of

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.660
millions of lives and
unspeakable suffering.”

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.980
“Big Tobacco Played Dirty
and Millions Died.”

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.380
How similar is Big Food?

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:15.540
I’ve talked about how
Big Food has used

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.280
the same tobacco
industry playbook.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.700
What about using the
anti-tobacco playbook

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.800
to counter the obesity crisis?

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.040
Tobacco is one of our great
public health victories.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:30.480
The share of adults who smoke
declined from 42 percent in 1965

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:33.420
down to just 15 percent by 2016.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:38.160
That’s about 5 out of 12 down
to less than 2 out of 12.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.860
Thanks to the decline,
cigarettes now only kill

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.840
about a half million
Americans every year,

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:46.260
whereas our diet now kills
tens of thousands more.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:51.720
Currently, the leading cause of death
in America is the American diet.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.740
Might we be able to use the same
strategies that were so successful

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.360
in the battle against Big Tobacco?

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:00.240
It may be no coincidence
that three of the most

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.940
cost-effective policy
interventions against obesity

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:06.480
seem to be taken straight
from the tobacco wars:

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:10.020
(1) taxes on unhealthy products;

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:11.790
(2) front-of-pack labeling; and

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.160
(3) a restriction of
advertising to children.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.760
Excise taxes on
cigarettes has been cited

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.120
as the single most effective
weapon in slashing smoking rates.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:25.800
A 25 cents per pack tax to help
deal with some of the societal costs

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.020
of smoking was
associated with as much as

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.300
a 9 percent decrease
in smoking rates.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.580
The World Health Organization
has estimated

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:36.000
that a 70 percent global
increase in the price could prevent

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.840
up to a quarter of all tobacco-
related deaths worldwide.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:44.400
Extending taxes on alcohol
and tobacco to foodstuffs

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.760
was proposed by none
other than Adam Smith

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.800
in his 1776 Wealth of Nations:

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.440
“Sugar, rum, and
tobacco, are commodities

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.660
which are nowhere
necessities of life,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:57.720
which have become objects of
almost universal consumption,

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:01.620
and which are, therefore, extremely
proper subjects of taxation.”

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.120
People have the right to smoke
and drink and eat fattening foods,

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:08.520
the logic goes, but perhaps
they should help defray

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.460
some of the publicly-funded
medical costs that result.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:16.080
Well, if that’s the case, why
not tax obese people directly?

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:20.280
Taxing people based on
their weight was proposed

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:23.760
at least as far back as 1904
in the British Medical Journal,

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.640
a kind of pounds-
for-pounds strategy

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:30.300
(actually only up to 7 shillings
and 6 pence per pound).

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:34.380
This was not only to
recompense public coffers,

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.300
but nudge behavioral change.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:40.560
“A tax on fat,” read the proposal,
“would also have an excellent effect

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.080
on the health of the nation
by bringing about a reform

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.620
of unwholesome habits
of eating and drinking.”

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.040
Even a penny-per-ounce tax
on sugar-sweetened beverages

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.440
could bring in more
than a billion dollars a year

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.240
in states like Texas
and California.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.720
A 10 percent tax on fattening
foods on a national level

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:01.080
could yield a half a
trillion dollars over ten years.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.620
Even if it was combined
with a subsidy that lowered

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.450
the cost of fruits and
vegetables by 10 percent,

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:10.200
it would be expected to net
hundreds of billions of dollars.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:14.100
But would it actually
change people’s habits?

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.920
Even a small price
differential (about 10 percent)

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.100
between leaded and
unleaded gas was able to shift

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.260
the entire auto
industry away from lead.

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:26.580
Could it also shift Americans
to apples from apple pie?

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.180
A systematic review of the
available evidence suggests

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:33.360
that dietary financial incentives
and disincentives work.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.640
The cheaper you make
fruits and vegetables,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:37.320
the more people say they’d buy,

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.420
and the more you
tax unhealthy foods,

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:40.980
the lower consumption drops.

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:44.640
Based on this kind of modeling,
a tax on saturated fat—

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:47.340
found mostly in fatty meat,
dairy, and junk—

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.760
could potentially save
thousands of lives a year.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.840
But wouldn’t such a tax
disproportionately affect the poor?

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.800
Yes, in that we would expect
them to benefit the most!

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.780
It’s like cigarette taxes.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:04.380
The classic tobacco industry
argument is that cigarette taxes

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:07.620
are “unfair” and “regressive,”
burdening the poor the most,

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:11.220
to which the public health community
responded “Cancer is unfair;”

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.680
cancer disproportionately
burdens the poor.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.980
So, taxes would be expected to
affect the greatest health gains

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.600
for the least well-off.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:22.320
The fact that the tobacco
industry fought tooth and nail

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:24.420
against cigarette taxes—
everything from

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:27.960
inventing front groups to
overtly buying off politicians—

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:31.320
suggests that taxes can indeed
shift consumption patterns,

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.960
but much of the evidence
on changing food behaviors

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.540
has not been based
on real-life data.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:40.680
You can put people through
fancy 3D supermarket simulators

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:44.040
and discover that a 25 percent
discount on fruits and veggies

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.980
appears to boost produce
purchasing by the same amount—

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:48.600
up to about two pounds a week.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:52.140
But virtual vegetables
don’t actually do you any good.

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.660
Does this work out
in the real world?

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:55.860
Apparently so.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:20:00.000
South Africa’s largest private
health insurer started offering

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.240
up to 25 percent cash back
on healthy food purchases

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:05.100
to hundreds of thousands
of households,

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:09.240
up to the U.S. equivalent
of $799 per month.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.880
Why would they give money away?

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.880
Because it apparently works—
increasing consumption

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.380
of fruits and vegetables
and whole grains,

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.660
while at the same time
decreasing the consumption

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:21.480
of foods high in added
salt, sugar, and fat,

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:24.300
including processed
meats and fast food.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:27.840
Why not just pay people to
lose weight directly?

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.900
A systematic review found
11 out of 12 studies

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:34.020
on financial incentives for weight
loss described positive results.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:38.940
The one that failed to find a benefit
of direct monetary inducements

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.820
was only offering $2.80 a day.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.200
With kids, you can get away
with just a nickel or a sticker

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:48.240
to get them to choose
dried fruit over a cookie

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:52.680
as an afterschool snack, though
as soon as the enticements ended,

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.660
so did the change in behavior.

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:57.720
Even if the incentives
have to be made permanent,

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:59.760
they might still
pay for themselves.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:03.420
In the U.S., every dollar spent
taxing processed foods or milk

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:07.080
might net an estimated $2
in healthcare cost savings.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:11.280
Every dollar spent making
vegetables cheaper could net $3

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.620
and subsidizing whole
grains might offer more

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.080
than a thousand percent
return on our investment.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.980
Even a 1 percent decrease in the
average price of all fruits and vegetables

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.820
might prevent nearly 10,000 heart
attacks and strokes every year.

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:28.620
What about taxes, though, which
tend to be less popular than subsidies?

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.140
In Europe, a number of
countries have instituted

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.180
taxes on sugary or salty foods,

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:36.960
but Denmark was the first to
introduce a tax on saturated fat.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.740
It only took agribusiness
about a year to squash it,

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.800
demonstrating how weak public
health professionals can be

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:46.020
when trying to tackle
corporate power.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.380
This was chalked up to
the “enormous imbalance”

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:52.320
between the political influence exerted
by the public health community

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:56.561
compared to the
industry’s lobbying might.

00:21:57.273 --> 00:21:59.940
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.940
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

